WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.725
Let’s work one more, so the steps stick.

2
00:00:02.975 --> 00:00:04.850
Follow it on screen.

3
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:11.450
A light stands one hundred feet above the water, and you’re on a flybridge with your eye sixteen feet up.

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.525
The light’s horizon.

5
00:00:13.775 --> 00:00:20.700
The square root of one hundred is ten, and ten times one point one seven is eleven point seven miles.

6
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:22.575
Your horizon.

7
00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:29.750
The square root of sixteen is four, and four times one point one seven is about four point seven miles.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.725
Add them: sixteen point four miles.

9
00:00:32.975 --> 00:00:35.200
That’s the geographic range.

10
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.150
Now the twist.

11
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.650
Tonight it’s hazy, and the luminous range is only twelve miles.

12
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.725
When do you see the light?

13
00:00:44.725 --> 00:00:46.650
At about twelve miles.

14
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.975
This time the air is the limit, not the earth.

15
00:00:50.225 --> 00:00:52.725
The lesser range always wins.

16
00:00:52.975 --> 00:00:56.025
Notice I picked heights with easy square roots.

17
00:00:56.275 --> 00:00:58.600
The exam usually does too.
